#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162953} :activity/id #uuid "94bfdff9-fb3b-5c84-9048-5e2a573a36a9" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049956 :topic/id #uuid "21353370-ac76-5b2e-8404-7422bec4c2e7" :topic/math-academy-id 3055} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162953 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163263 :tutorial/content "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable whose probability density function is known. Suppose we want to define a new random variable $Y$ as\n\n$$\nY = u(X),\n$$\n\nwhere $u = u(X)$ is a function of $X.$ How do we find the probability density function of $Y?$\n\nTo find the PDF of $Y,$ we can use the **distribution function method**. The distribution function method consists of two steps:\n\n- First, we find an expression for $F_Y$ in terms of $F_X,$ where $F_Y$ and $F_X$ are the cumulative distribution functions of $Y$ and $X,$ respectively.\n\n- Then, we differentiate $F_Y$ to get the probability density function $f_Y$ of $Y.$\n\nIn this lesson, we'll assume that the function $u$ is strictly monotonic (i.e., strictly increasing or decreasing). The case where $u$ is not monotonic will be dealt with in a future lesson.\n\nLet's see an example of the distribution function method in action." :tutorial/id #uuid "6e59563a-5641-533c-8434-d07d587129e0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5355 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "545a2e11-b3d3-5c57-9ddf-9aec604aff0b" :step/math-academy-id 33485 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163098}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162957 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162046 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161846 :question/id #uuid "6276f196-80de-58b7-acb6-a715ee955f6d" :question/math-academy-id "e-9073"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2008912a-15de-5248-a9b5-c375eb3dc292" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049573} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049986} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050580} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050750} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051084}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162248} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162294} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552403} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552425} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552463} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552467}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the PDF of a Random Variable Under an Affine Transformation"} :step/id #uuid "e0e38c10-faac-57fb-b114-7d58e7238835" :step/math-academy-id 33840 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162958}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162958 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162047 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161847 :question/id #uuid "b9f862ab-0544-503e-8627-3de73ec60594" :question/math-academy-id "e-9074"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "93f1f64d-e88e-5f60-8655-459b15eaf715" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048553} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049313} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049435} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049528} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049649} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050126} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050271}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162246} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552417} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552446} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552458} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552522}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the PDF of a Random Variable Under a Nonlinear Transformation"} :step/id #uuid "ec2fc57f-ed17-5553-9197-3e0d2cd11097" :step/math-academy-id 33841} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163098 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163339 :tutorial/content "Suppose the continuous random variable $X$ is defined as\n\n![](images/88/88103878042469595fc8f8243958b44b128c0d38465bfe93a31f83afd6988300.svg)\nLet $Y= 1+2X.$ Let's use the distribution function method to find the probability density function of $Y.$\n\nFirst, note that $Y = 1+2X$ is a strictly increasing function of $X.$ Moreover, for $x > 0,$ we have $y > 1.$\n\n![](images/54/544e551755f4c8a575ef227b77fe520deec203f08654eec31109e496141aa91c.png)\n\nLet's consider $y > 1.$ We write down the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ and then use our transformation to isolate $X$ in the parentheses. This will allow us to write $F_Y$ in terms of $F_X\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P(2X+1\\le y) \\\\  & =P(2X\\le y-1) \\\\  & =P(X\\le \\frac{y-1}{2}) \\\\  & ={F}_{X}(\\frac{y-1}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the PDF $f_Y(y)$ for $y > 1,$ we differentiate $F_Y$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({F}_{X}(\\frac{y-1}{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\frac{y-1}{2})\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}(\\frac{y-1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}(\\frac{y-1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(\\frac{y-1}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of $f_X,$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(\\frac{y-1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {e}^{-(y-1)/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{(1-y)/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the PDF of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{(1-y)/2}, & \\,y>1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt's easy (and often worthwhile) to check that this is a valid probability density function." :tutorial/id #uuid "6eea312a-622e-5ce1-ae71-45e4cc089144" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6387 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "4b31cb7f-dcfe-50f4-9a5b-26957ca91f13" :step/math-academy-id 38639 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162957}}] :activity/title "The Distribution Function Method" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162191}]}
